    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 89\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 7177, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to elim-\n      inating  unconstitutional  language  relating  to the pre-audit of\n      expenditures from the state insurance  fund  by  the  state  comp-\n      troller\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would amend the Workers' Compensation Law to eliminate the\nNew York State Insurance Fund's (NYSIF) long-standing exemption  enacted\nunder  the workers' compensation reform law of 1996 from certain pre-au-\ndit requirements. This bill, substantially  similar  versions  of  which\nhave  been  vetoed  in  previous years, remains problematic and seeks to\ncure a problem that does not exist while at the same time would  reverse\nyears of modernization efforts of the worker's compensation system.\n \n  In  1996,  the  workers'  compensation  system was reformed to protect\ninjured workers and deliver wage replacement benefits in a fair, equita-\nble and efficient manner,  while  reducing  time-consuming  bureaucratic\ndelays.  The  now outdated system included full pre-auditing approval of\ninsurance payments by the State Comptroller. This process was cumbersome\nand inefficient, and resulted in NYSIF incurring a high rate  of  penal-\nties  for  late payment of awards to injured workers which significantly\nand directly increased costs to NYSIF and its policyholders. But reforms\nhave worked: for the first quarter of 2021, NYSIF's  on-time  compliance\nrate for initial payments to injured workers was nearly 100%.\n \n  Through  its  heavily staffed audit division, which processes over 2.5\nmillion payment transactions annually, NYSIF has  robust  and  effective\ninternal  controls  to  audit  its  expenditures and payments and ensure\naccurate on-time payment performance. The re-establishment of full  pre-\nauditing  of NYSIF insurance payments by the Comptroller would duplicate\nNYSIF's own pre-auditing systems, increase delays in payment processing,\nerode NYSIF's ability to make on time payments to injured  workers,  and\nthwart  NYSIF's  statutory  mission  of  providing worker's compensation\ninsurance to New Yorkers at the lowest possible cost.\n \n  In an effort to compromise, amendments to the bill were offered  which\nwould  have  imposed  time  limits  on  the auditing process in order to\nensure that the State Comptroller's pre-audit process  would  not  delay\nlost  wage  or  medical payments to injured workers. The amendments were\nnot agreed to. We cannot risk imposing additional hardship  on  workers,\nparticularly  injured workers, during this time of economic uncertainty.\nFor these reasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  This bill is disapproved.                      (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
